WN.lb(33,{"mt_Wpvuz3mvBq":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A dinosaur's hip was {=4*a} cm high. The hip is about 4 footprints high. About how long was its footprint, in cm?","answer":"a","hint":"Share the hip height into 4 equal footprints.","explain":"{=4*a} ÷ 4 = {a}. The footprint was about {a} cm long.","params":{"a":[10,40]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A footprint is 30 cm long. {a} footprints fit in the gap between prints. How long is the gap, in cm?","answer":"30*a","hint":"Count in 30s, once for each footprint.","explain":"{a} × 30 = {=30*a}. The gap is {=30*a} cm.","params":{"a":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A giant's prints are 180 cm apart. Its footprint is 60 cm long. Was it running?","choices":["No: only 3 footprints fit, so it walked","Yes: 180 cm is a long way","Yes: giants always run"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the gap in footprints, not in cm.","explain":"180 ÷ 60 = 3 footprints. A gap of about 3 footprints is a walking step, even for a giant."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which print was made by a long-necked giant?","choices":["A big round print with a small half-moon in front","Three thin toes with claws","Three fat, blunt toes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Long-necked giants walked on four legs and their feet were round.","explain":"Long-necked giants left big round back-foot prints with small half-moon front-foot prints."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What can a fossil trackway NOT tell us?","choices":["What colour the dinosaur was","How big it was","If it walked or ran"],"answer":0,"hint":"A footprint is only the shape of a foot pressed into mud.","explain":"A print shows size, toe shape, speed and direction. It cannot show colour."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Many trackways cross each other and point in every direction. What is the best idea?","choices":["They walked by at different times","A herd moved together","One dinosaur went round in circles"],"answer":0,"hint":"A herd moves the same way, side by side.","explain":"Crossing trackways that point different ways were probably made at different times, not by one group."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Two prints are the same shape. One is twice as long. What does that tell you?","choices":["Its maker was about twice as tall at the hip","Its maker was running","Its maker was younger"],"answer":0,"hint":"The hip is about 4 footprints high, whatever the size.","explain":"A footprint twice as long means a hip about twice as high, so a bigger dinosaur."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam says the prints are far apart, so the dinosaur must have been running. What should he check?","choices":["How many footprints fit in the gap","The colour of the rock","How deep the rock is"],"answer":0,"hint":"Big animals take big steps even when they walk.","explain":"He should count the gap in footprints. About 3 is walking; 6 or more is running."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"How did the footprints last for millions of years?","choices":["The mud hardened into rock","Dinosaurs pressed them into rock","Rain filled them with ice"],"answer":0,"hint":"The ground was soft when the dinosaur walked on it.","explain":"The prints were made in soft mud or sand. It was covered over and slowly hardened into rock."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A fossil footprint is {a} cm long. The hip is about 4 footprints high. About how high was the hip, in cm?","answer":"4*a","hint":"Stack 4 footprints on top of each other. Work out 4 lots of {a}.","explain":"4 × {a} = {=4*a}. The hip was about {=4*a} cm high.","params":{"a":[10,50]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A footprint is 20 cm long. The gap between two prints is {=20*a} cm. How many footprints fit in the gap?","answer":"a","hint":"Count in 20s until you reach {=20*a}.","explain":"{=20*a} ÷ 20 = {a}. {a} footprints fit in the gap.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A print has three thin toes with sharp points at the ends. Who made it?","choices":["A meat-eater","A long-necked giant","A plant-eater with blunt toes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sharp points at the toe tips are claw marks.","explain":"Thin toes with sharp claw marks were made by a meat-eater, such as Allosaurus."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"About 3 footprints fit in each gap of a trackway. What was the dinosaur doing?","choices":["Walking","Running fast","Standing still"],"answer":0,"hint":"About 2 to 4 footprints in a gap is a normal step. 6 or more is a long, fast step.","explain":"About 3 footprints in each gap is a walking step. Running steps are 6 or more footprints long."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Five trackways run side by side, all going the same way. What is the best idea?","choices":["A group moved together","One dinosaur walked five times","They were made by rain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the direction and the spacing.","explain":"Trackways side by side, going the same way and evenly spaced, suggest a group moving together."}],"explain":{"kid":["Long ago, dinosaurs walked on soft mud. Their feet left prints. The mud turned to rock, and the prints were kept. A line of prints made by one dinosaur is called a trackway.","Look at the toes first. Thin toes with sharp claw marks were made by a meat-eater. Fat, blunt toes were made by a plant-eater that walked on two legs. Big round prints were made by a long-necked giant.","A big print means a big dinosaur. Its hip was about 4 footprints high. So a print 30 cm long means a hip about 120 cm high.","Now count how many footprints fit in the gap between two prints. About 3 means it was walking. 6 or more means it was running. A giant takes big steps even when it walks, so always count in footprints."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. Children use trace fossils as evidence. A trackway is a series of footprints from one animal. Key words: trace fossil, footprint, trackway, stride, theropod (meat-eater), ornithopod and sauropod (plant-eaters), evidence, infer.","The size rule comes from Alexander (1976): hip height is roughly 4 times footprint length (between 4 and 6, depending on the kind of dinosaur). A long stride compared with hip height means a faster gait, so scientists compare the gap with the size of the foot.","A common mistake is to say that prints far apart always mean running. A big dinosaur walking can leave wider gaps than a small one running, so the gap has to be counted in footprint lengths. Another slip is to read every crowded track site as a herd: trackways that cross and point different ways were probably made at different times. Parallel trackways going the same way, as at Davenport Ranch in Texas, suggest a group.","In the lab your child sorts six prints by toe shape, counts 10 cm squares to find each footprint's length and its hip height, fills the gap between prints with footprints to decide walking or running, judges three track sites seen from above, and checks a robot that says wide gaps always mean running and that prints show colour."]},"familyTip":"Walk, then run, across damp sand or a wet path and look at your own trackway. Count how many of your footprints fit in each gap. Then measure your foot and your leg up to your hip: is your hip about 4 footprints high too?","keyIdea":"A trackway is a line of fossil footprints left by one dinosaur as it moved. The shape of the toes shows what kind of dinosaur made it. The size of a print shows how big it was: the hip is about 4 footprints high. The gap between prints, counted in footprints, shows if it walked or ran. Trackways side by side going the same way show a group moving together.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What can a trackway tell us? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["A trackway can show this","A trackway cannot show this"],"items":[["How big the dinosaur was",0],["What colour it was",1],["If it walked or ran",0],["What sound it made",1],["If it walked in a group",0],["Its name",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtrack","caption":"Flagship lab: sort prints by toe shape, find hip height, count footprints in a gap to tell walking from running, spot a herd, check a robot.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_1dXhJp6qLJ":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A big coprolite is full of broken bone. Who most likely made it?","choices":["A big meat-eater","A big plant-eater","A tiny insect"],"answer":0,"hint":"Big tells you the size. What does the bone tell you?","explain":"A big meat-eater. Size points to a big animal, and the bone shows it ate meat."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How do scientists see what is inside a coprolite?","choices":["Cut thin slices or scan it","Smell it","Melt it"],"answer":0,"hint":"A coprolite is hard stone. How do you look inside a stone?","explain":"They cut thin slices to look at under a microscope, or scan it without cutting."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A coprolite has rotted wood and seeds inside. What does it tell us?","choices":["The animal ate plants","The animal ate meat","Nothing: it is just a rock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where do wood and seeds come from?","explain":"Wood and seeds come from plants, so a plant-eater made it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Can scientists be sure which dinosaur did a poo?","choices":["No, it is a best guess from clues","Yes, always","Yes, the name is inside"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did anyone see the dinosaur do it?","explain":"No. Nobody saw it, so scientists use clues and say “most likely”."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A coprolite is {n} cm long. A ruler is 30 cm long. How many cm longer than the ruler is the coprolite?","answer":"n-30","hint":"The coprolite is longer than the ruler. Find the difference: take the ruler’s 30 cm away from {n} cm.","explain":"{n} − 30 = {=n-30} cm longer than the ruler.","params":{"n":[35,44]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which comes first when poo becomes a coprolite?","choices":["Mud covers it fast","It turns to stone","Minerals soak in"],"answer":0,"hint":"What must happen before it can rot or get eaten?","explain":"First mud covers it fast. Then minerals soak in for a very long time, and it turns to stone."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"The 44 cm coprolite from Canada was full of bone from a young plant-eater. What does that tell us?","choices":["A big meat-eater ate a young plant-eater","A plant-eater ate bones","It was a dinosaur egg"],"answer":0,"hint":"Whose bones were inside, and who eats bones?","explain":"A big meat-eater, most likely T. rex, ate a young plant-eating dinosaur."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A slice shows {n} bits of bone and {k} fish scales. How many bits is that in all?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Add the bone bits and the fish scales.","explain":"{n} and {k} make {=n+k} bits. This animal ate fish and other bony animals.","params":{"n":[3,9],"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Who first saw fish bones and scales inside these stones at Lyme Regis?","choices":["Mary Anning","A T. rex","A deep-sea diver"],"answer":0,"hint":"She was a famous fossil hunter on the coast of England.","explain":"Mary Anning noticed them. William Buckland then named them coprolites in 1829."},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Which one can a scientist say about a coprolite full of bone?","choices":["The animal ate bone","It was T. rex for sure","The dinosaur was angry"],"answer":0,"hint":"Only say what the clues show.","explain":"The bone inside shows the animal ate bone. The name and feelings are not shown."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is a coprolite?","choices":["Poo that turned to stone","Soft, smelly old poo","A dinosaur egg"],"answer":0,"hint":"Copro means poo. Lite means stone.","explain":"A coprolite is fossilised dung: poo that was buried fast and slowly turned to stone."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A coprolite is {n} cm long. Another is {k} cm longer. How long is the second one, in cm?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Start at {n} cm and add {k} cm.","explain":"{n} cm and {k} cm more make {=n+k} cm. A longer coprolite usually came from a bigger animal.","params":{"n":[10,40],"k":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A coprolite has fish scales inside. What did the animal eat?","choices":["Fish","Plants","Rocks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where do scales like that come from?","explain":"Fish scales and fish bones mean the animal ate fish."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A scientist cuts {n} slices from one coprolite and {k} slices from another. How many slices is that?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Count the slices from the first one, then count on.","explain":"{n} slices and {k} more make {=n+k} slices to look at under a microscope.","params":{"n":[2,6],"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why must dung be covered fast to become a fossil?","choices":["So it does not rot or get eaten first","So it can dry in the sun","So it stays soft"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to poo that is left lying about?","explain":"Dung left out rots, crumbles or gets eaten. Covered fast by mud, it can slowly turn to stone."}],"explain":{"kid":["A coprolite is poo that turned to stone. It is a kind of fossil. The poo had to be covered by mud very fast. Then, over a very long time, minerals soaked in and made it hard. It does not smell now.","Scientists cut a coprolite into thin slices, or scan it, to look inside. They find tiny bits of what the animal ate. Bone means it ate meat. Wood, seeds or leaves mean it ate plants. Fish scales mean it ate fish.","In Canada, scientists found a coprolite 44 cm long. It was full of broken bone from a young plant-eating dinosaur. A big poo full of bone came from a big meat-eater. T. rex was the big meat-eater that lived there.","But nobody saw it happen. A poo has no name on it. So scientists say T. rex “most likely” made it. They use clues to make a best guess."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. The word coprolite comes from Greek: kopros means dung and lithos means stone. William Buckland coined it in 1829. Mary Anning had noticed that odd stones found inside ichthyosaur skeletons at Lyme Regis held fish bones and scales when broken open. The lesson fits the National Curriculum in England: Year 3 rocks and fossils, and the Year 6 idea that fossils give evidence about life long ago.","Dung only becomes a fossil if it is buried fast, before it rots or is eaten. Minerals then fill it and replace it. Meat-eaters’ dung is kept more often, because the phosphate from bone helps it harden. Scientists cut thin slices or use scans and microscopes. Inside they find bone, plant tissue, seeds, pollen, scales and even worm eggs. In 1998 Karen Chin and her team described a coprolite 44 cm long from Saskatchewan. It was 30 to 50% bone from a young plant-eating dinosaur. Its size, its contents and the animals of that time and place point to Tyrannosaurus rex. In 2007 Chin found rotted conifer wood in dung thought to come from duck-billed dinosaurs.","Common mistakes: thinking a coprolite is still soft poo, or that any dropping left lying about turns to stone; assuming a big dropping means a plant-eater; and treating the maker as certain. A coprolite rarely names its maker. Scientists reason from size, shape, contents and which animals lived there, and they say “most likely”.","In the lab your child orders the steps from fresh poo to stone, cuts a coprolite open and names the bone, seed, wood and fish scale inside, sorts coprolites by diet, measures the 44 cm coprolite on a ruler and picks its likely maker, and sorts claims into what we can say and what is too sure."]},"familyTip":"Next time you see animal droppings on a walk, ask: what did this animal eat? Look for seeds, fur or bits of shell, without touching. Then ask how sure you are, and what would make you surer. If you visit a museum, look for coprolites in the fossil cases: many museums have one.","keyIdea":"A coprolite is fossilised dung: animal poo that was buried fast and slowly turned to stone. Scientists cut thin slices or scan coprolites to see inside. Bits of bone mean a meat-eater, wood, seeds or leaves mean a plant-eater, and fish scales mean a fish-eater. A coprolite 44 cm long from Canada was full of bone from a young plant-eating dinosaur, so a big meat-eater, most likely T. rex, made it. Clues give a best guess, not a sure answer, because nobody saw who did the poo.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What did the animal eat? Drag each coprolite, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Plant-eater","Meat-eater"],"items":[["Rotted wood inside",0],["Broken bits of bone",1],["Seeds and leaf bits",0],["A tooth and crushed bone",1],["Pollen from plants",0],["Bone from a young dinosaur",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdung","caption":"Flagship lab: turn poo into stone in order, cut a coprolite open, sort coprolites by diet, measure one, and decide how sure we can be.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_u0TeRwRII_":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Were all dinosaurs bigger than a bus?","choices":["No, some were as small as a chicken","Yes, all of them","Yes, all meat-eaters were"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about Compsognathus.","explain":"Dinosaurs came in many sizes. Compsognathus was about chicken-sized."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How big was a real Velociraptor?","choices":["About as big as a turkey","Taller than a grown-up","As long as a bus"],"answer":0,"hint":"Films make it bigger than it was.","explain":"A real Velociraptor was about turkey-sized, reaching about knee height on a grown-up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"T. rex was about 12 m long. A bus is about 11 m. T. rex was ...","choices":["about as long as a bus","much shorter than a bus","3 buses long"],"answer":0,"hint":"12 m and 11 m are only 1 m apart.","explain":"12 m is close to 11 m, so T. rex was about as long as a bus."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"On a see-saw, T. rex goes down and 1 big elephant goes up. Which is true?","choices":["T. rex was heavier than the elephant","T. rex was lighter than the elephant","T. rex was taller than a house"],"answer":0,"hint":"The heavier side goes down.","explain":"The heavy side goes down, so T. rex was heavier than 1 big elephant."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A real Velociraptor was about 50 cm tall at the hips. A child is about {a} cm tall. How many cm taller is the child?","answer":"a-50","hint":"Take 50 away from the child's height.","explain":"{a} take away 50 is {=a-50}, so the child is {=a-50} cm taller. Real raptors were small.","params":{"a":[100,120]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word fits? \"Brachiosaurus was ___ a house.\"","choices":["taller than","shorter than","lighter than"],"answer":0,"hint":"It held its head about 12 m up.","explain":"A house is about 8 m tall and Brachiosaurus about 12 m, so it was taller than a house."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Diplodocus was longer than Brachiosaurus. Was it taller too?","choices":["No, Brachiosaurus was taller","Yes, longer means taller","They were the same height"],"answer":0,"hint":"Long and tall measure different ways.","explain":"Longer is about length and taller is about height. Brachiosaurus was taller."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Compsognathus was about 70 cm long. Your arm is about {a} cm long. How many cm longer was Compsognathus?","answer":"70-a","hint":"Take your arm length away from 70.","explain":"70 take away {a} is {=70-a}, so Compsognathus was {=70-a} cm longer than your arm.","params":{"a":[40,60]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A bus is about 11 m long. A long-necked dinosaur was about {a} m long. How many metres longer than the bus was it?","answer":"a-11","hint":"Take the bus length, 11 m, away from the dinosaur's length.","explain":"{a} take away 11 is {=a-11}, so the dinosaur was about {=a-11} m longer than the bus.","params":{"a":[20,26]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A big elephant weighs about 6 tonnes. How many tonnes do {a} big elephants weigh?","answer":"a*6","hint":"Add 6 tonnes for each elephant.","explain":"{a} elephants of about 6 tonnes each make about {=a*6} tonnes.","params":{"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which dinosaur was about the size of a chicken?","choices":["Compsognathus","T. rex","Diplodocus"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the smallest one in the lab.","explain":"Compsognathus was only about 70 cm long and weighed about 3 kg, like a chicken."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Diplodocus was the longest. Brachiosaurus was ...","choices":["taller","longer","smaller than a bus"],"answer":0,"hint":"Brachiosaurus held its head high up, like a giraffe.","explain":"Brachiosaurus was taller, at about 12 m. Diplodocus was longer. Longest is not tallest."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"In a book, T. rex and Velociraptor are drawn the same size. What is true?","choices":["T. rex was much bigger","They were the same size","Velociraptor was bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Book pictures are drawn to fit the page.","explain":"T. rex was about 12 m long. Velociraptor was about 2 m, the size of a turkey. Pictures are not real size."}],"explain":{"kid":["Not all dinosaurs were big. Compsognathus was about as big as a chicken. Velociraptor was about as big as a turkey.","Some were giants. T. rex was about as long as a bus. Diplodocus was more than 2 buses long. Brachiosaurus was taller than a house.","In a book, a tiny dinosaur and a giant can look the same size. To see real size, put them next to a bus, a house or you.","Long is not the same as tall. Diplodocus was the longest, but Brachiosaurus was taller. We say longer, taller or heavier to compare."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7. Young children love naming dinosaurs, and size is a good way into comparing and measuring. Key words: size, length, height, weight, longer than, shorter than, taller than, heavier than, about, fossil.","Sizes come from fossil skeletons and are estimates, so say 'about'. Rounded figures from the Natural History Museum: Compsognathus 0.7 m long and 3 kg; Velociraptor about 2 m long with its tail, but only 0.5 m tall at the hips; Triceratops about 9 m; T. rex 12 m long and about 7 tonnes; Diplodocus about 26 m. Brachiosaurus stood about 12 to 13 m tall. A double-decker bus is about 11 m long.","Common mistakes: thinking every dinosaur was huge, trusting book pictures drawn to fill the page, and thinking the longest must be the tallest. Films are another trap: their raptors are as tall as a grown-up, but a real Velociraptor reached about knee height. Be careful with the words too: longer, taller and heavier compare different things.","In the lab your child moves book pictures onto a road beside a bus, measures Diplodocus and Brachiosaurus, picks the animal closest in size to a real Velociraptor and Compsognathus, weighs T. rex and Diplodocus against elephants on a see-saw, and fills sentence gaps with size words."]},"familyTip":"Measure a dinosaur outside. Pace out 12 big steps on a path for T. rex and stand at each end. Then try Compsognathus: less than one big step. Ask which dinosaur would fit in your room, and which would not fit in your street.","keyIdea":"Dinosaurs came in a huge range of sizes. Compsognathus was about the size of a chicken, and Velociraptor about the size of a turkey. T. rex was about as long as a bus, Diplodocus more than 2 buses long, and Brachiosaurus taller than a house. Book pictures are not real size, so compare with things you know. Longest is not always tallest, and longer, taller and heavier compare different things.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Longer or shorter than a bus? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Longer than a bus","Shorter than a bus"],"items":[["Diplodocus",0],["Compsognathus",1],["Brachiosaurus",0],["Velociraptor",1],["Triceratops",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdinosize","caption":"Flagship lab: put book dinosaurs on a road beside a bus, measure long and tall, size up a film raptor, weigh with elephants, pick size words.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_dpM1l5IOk6":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A fossil can be…","choices":["a bone shape in rock","a new plastic toy","a cartoon drawing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fossils are preserved in rock from living things.","explain":"Bones, teeth, footprints and eggs in rock can be fossils. Toys and cartoons are not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Dinosaurs lived…","choices":["millions of years ago","last weekend","only in books with no fossils"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think deep time, before people.","explain":"Dinosaurs lived millions of years ago. Fossils prove they were real, not only stories."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A footprint in rock is useful because…","choices":["an animal once stood there","someone drew it yesterday","it is made of metal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Footprints can be trace fossils.","explain":"A footprint fossil shows an animal once stood there. Fresh drawings and metal are not fossils."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A dragon in a story is…","choices":["make-believe","a kind of fossil dinosaur","proof people rode dinosaurs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Stories are not the same as fossil evidence.","explain":"Dragons are make-believe. Dinosaurs are real animals known from fossils."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A museum shelf shows {n} fossil teeth. How many fossil teeth?","answer":"n","hint":"Count the fossil teeth on the shelf.","explain":"The shelf shows {n} fossil teeth — real evidence in rock.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{n} dig boxes each hold {k} fossil bones. How many bones in all?","answer":"n*k","hint":"Equal groups: {n} boxes of {k}.","explain":"{n} × {k} = {=n*k} fossil bones in all.","params":{"n":[2,4],"k":[2,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why dig carefully for fossils?","choices":["so you do not break the shape in the rock","because rock is food","because fossils melt in air"],"answer":0,"hint":"The shape in the rock is the evidence.","explain":"Careful digging protects the fossil shape. Rock is not food; fossils do not melt in air."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which list is all fossil evidence?","choices":["bone in rock, tooth in rock, footprint in rock","toy, cartoon, soft pillow","metal coin, plastic model, sticker"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fossils come from living things trapped in rock.","explain":"Bone, tooth and footprint in rock are fossil evidence. Toys and coins are not."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Dinosaurs were…","choices":["real animals long ago","only plastic toys","the same as dragons"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about fossils in rock, not shop toys.","explain":"Dinosaurs were real animals millions of years ago. Toys are models; dragons are stories."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"We know dinosaurs lived because of…","choices":["fossils in rock","cartoons on TV","toy shops"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do scientists dig from rock layers?","explain":"Fossils — bones, teeth, footprints, eggs in rock — are the evidence. Cartoons and toys are not evidence."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A dig finds {n} fossil bones and {k} plastic toys. How many fossil finds?","answer":"n","hint":"Count only the fossils, not the toys.","explain":"There are {n} fossil bones. The {k} plastic toys are not fossils.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A tray has {n} finds. {k} are toys. How many could be fossils?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract the toys from the total finds.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} finds that are not toys and might be fossils.","params":{"n":[5,8],"k":[1,3]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"People and dinosaurs…","choices":["did not live at the same time","went to school together","built toy shops together"],"answer":0,"hint":"Dinosaurs lived millions of years before people.","explain":"Dinosaurs died out long before people. Fossils sit in much older rock layers."}],"explain":{"kid":["Dinosaurs were real animals. They lived on Earth a very long time ago — millions of years before people.","We know because of fossils. A fossil can be a bone, a tooth, a footprint or an egg shape trapped in rock.","A plastic toy dinosaur is not a fossil. It was made in a factory. A story dragon is make-believe.","Scientists dig carefully for fossils. The rock layers show that dinosaurs lived long before us."],"adult":["Year 1 and 2 science: animals that lived long ago. Fossils are clues. Words to use: dinosaur, fossil, evidence, extinct, long ago, footprint, bone, model.","Common mistakes: thinking dinosaurs are only toys; mixing them with dragons; saying people lived with dinosaurs; calling any bone a fossil; thinking all dinosaurs were huge meat-eaters.","In the lab your child puts dinosaurs before people on a line, digs up fossil clues, sorts real clues from toys, matches finds, then faces traps for those slips."]},"familyTip":"At a museum or in a book, ask: 'Is this a real fossil bone, or a model?' Point to a footprint cast and say 'an animal once stood here.'","keyIdea":"Dinosaurs were real animals that lived on Earth long ago — millions of years before people. Fossils (bones, teeth, footprints and eggs in rock) are the evidence. Toy dinosaurs and story dragons are not fossils.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Real evidence or make-believe? Drag each item, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fossil evidence","Not fossil evidence"],"items":[["bone shape in rock",0],["plastic toy dinosaur",1],["footprint in rock",0],["cartoon dragon",1],["tooth in rock",0],["stuffed dinosaur toy",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdino","caption":"Flagship lab: place dinosaurs in deep time, dig fossil clues, sort evidence from toys, match finds, and reject myths.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_6Z42wJaKYG":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which of these is a fossil?","choices":["A footprint of a dinosaur kept in rock","A pebble rounded by the sea","A shell from a snail in the garden today"],"answer":0,"hint":"A fossil is the remains or trace of a living thing from long ago, kept in rock.","explain":"The footprint is a trace of a living thing from long ago, kept in rock. A pebble was never alive, and today's snail shell is not old."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Layer X lies under layer Y in a cliff. Neither has been tipped over. Which is older?","choices":["Layer X","Layer Y","They are the same age"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which layer settled first?","explain":"X settled first and Y piled on top later, so X is older."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Fossil coral is found in a hill in England. What does it tell you?","choices":["The place was once a warm, shallow sea","Coral can grow on dry hills","England is a warm sea today"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about where coral grows: on land or in the sea?","explain":"Coral only grows in warm, shallow sea water. The rock formed in such a sea long ago."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Fossil tree leaves are found in rock in icy Antarctica. What can you work out?","choices":["It was once warm enough for trees","Trees can grow on ice","The leaves fell there last year"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can trees grow on ice? How old are fossils?","explain":"Trees need warmer land than Antarctica has today, and fossils are very old. So Antarctica was once warm enough for forests."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Sand settles on a seabed at about {a} cm every 1,000 years. How thick is the sand after {b},000 years, in cm?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Find how many lots of 1,000 years there are.","explain":"{b},000 years is {b} lots of 1,000 years. {b} × {a} cm = {=a*b} cm.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[5,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A rock layer formed {a} million years ago. The layer on top of it formed {b} million years later. How many million years ago did the top layer form?","answer":"a-b","hint":"The top layer is younger, so it formed fewer years ago.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}. The top layer formed {=a-b} million years ago, so it is younger than the one below.","params":{"a":[40,60],"b":[5,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A cliff has coral at the bottom, ferns in the middle and sand dunes at the top. Which story does it tell?","choices":["Sea, then swamp forest, then desert","Desert, then swamp forest, then sea","All three at the same time"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the layers from the bottom up.","explain":"The bottom layer is oldest, so first there was a warm sea, then a swamp forest, then a desert."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A mammoth tooth is found in rock. What was the place like when the mammoth lived?","choices":["A cold, icy grassland","A warm, shallow sea","A rainforest"],"answer":0,"hint":"Woolly mammoths had thick fur and ate grass.","explain":"Woolly mammoths were built for the cold and ate grass, so the place was a cold grassland in the Ice Age."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A rock has fossils of fish and sea shells in it. What was this place like when the rock formed?","choices":["It was under the sea","It was a hot desert","It was a snowy mountain"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do fish and shellfish need to live?","explain":"Fish and shellfish need sea water, so the rock formed under the sea, even if it is on land now."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Mud settles on a lake bed at about {a} mm each year. How many millimetres of mud build up in {b} years?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Multiply the mm for one year by the number of years.","explain":"{a} mm × {b} = {=a*b} mm. Slow layers like this, piled over millions of years, become rock.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[10,20]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A cliff has {n} layers. They are numbered from 1 at the bottom. Which layer number is the youngest?","answer":"n*1","hint":"New layers settle on top of older ones.","explain":"The top layer settled last, so layer {n} is the youngest and layer 1 is the oldest.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Fossil sea shells are found high up on a mountain. What is the best explanation?","choices":["The seabed rock was pushed up over millions of years","A big flood carried them up","Shellfish used to live on mountains"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fossils show where the rock formed. Can rock move?","explain":"The shells formed in seabed rock. Over millions of years that rock was pushed up, so the shells went up with it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"The oldest horse fossils have 4 toes on each front foot. What does that show?","choices":["Horses have changed over a very long time","Each old horse lost its toes by running","Fossils are not real animals"],"answer":0,"hint":"Could one horse change its own feet during its life?","explain":"The change from 4 toes to 1 took millions of years and many generations. One horse cannot change its own toes."}],"explain":{"kid":["A fossil is what is left of a living thing from long ago, kept in rock. It can be a shell, a bone, a tooth, a leaf or a footprint. A fossil is a clue. Ask what the living thing needed. Coral only grows in warm, shallow sea. Ferns need warm, wet ground. Woolly mammoths had thick fur for the cold. So a fossil tells you what the place was like back then.","Rock forms in layers. Mud and sand settle, and new layers pile on top. So the lowest layer is the oldest. Read a cliff from the bottom up, and you read the story of that place in order.","Rocks can move. Sea shells are found near the top of Mount Everest. That rock was once the seabed. Over millions of years it was pushed up into a mountain. Fossils show where a rock formed, not where it is now.","Fossils show that life has changed. The oldest horse fossils had 4 toes on each front foot. Later ones had 3. Horses today have 1 toe, a hoof. This change took millions of years and many, many generations."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 11. NGSS 3-LS4-1 asks pupils to use fossil data as evidence of the living things and places of long ago. In England, Year 6 pupils learn that living things have changed over time. They also learn that fossils tell us about life millions of years ago. The lab uses three kinds of reasoning. What a living thing needed tells you about the place. The order of layers tells you which is older. A series of fossils shows change over time.","Facts used. In layers that have not been tipped, the lower rock is older (Steno's rule of superposition). The top of Everest is limestone about 450 to 470 million years old. It holds fossils of trilobites and other sea animals (Natural History Museum, USGS). India pushing into Asia raised it, starting about 50 million years ago. Wenlock Edge in Shropshire is a fossil coral reef from a warm sea about 430 million years ago. Fossil leaves and tree stumps are found in Antarctica. Early horses such as Eohippus, about 50 million years ago, had 4 toes on each front foot. Mesohippus, about 35 million years ago, had 3. One-toed horses date from about 4 million years ago. The horse family tree branched; the lab shows three points, not a ladder.","Common mistakes. Children often think the top layer is the oldest. They read a fossil as a clue about the place today, not when the rock formed. They explain sea shells on a mountain with a flood. They think an animal changes its own body by using it, in one lifetime, rather than a group changing over many generations. They think every old bone is a dinosaur. In the lab each slip gets a message that names it. The cliff is a simple model: real layers can be folded or tipped, so the challenge says 'neither has been tipped over'."]},"familyTip":"Make a 'cliff' in a clear jar. Pour in a layer of sand, drop in a small shell, then add soil, then rice. Ask which layer went in first and which object is the 'oldest'. Then tip the jar on its side and talk about how real rock can be pushed up or tilted, but the order of the layers still tells the story.","keyIdea":"A fossil is the remains or trace of a living thing from long ago, such as a shell, bone, tooth, leaf or footprint, kept in rock. Fossils are clues. What the living thing needed tells you what the place was like: coral means a warm, shallow sea, ferns mean warm, wet land. Rock forms in layers, and lower layers are older, so a cliff can be read from the bottom up like a story. Fossils show where a rock formed, not where it is now: sea shells near the top of Mount Everest are in old seabed rock that was pushed up. Old fossils that differ from living things, like horses with 4 toes, show that life has changed over millions of years.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What was this place like when the fossil was alive? Drag each fossil to a box, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Once under the sea","Once dry or wet land"],"items":[["Fossil coral",0],["Fossil shark tooth",0],["Fossil sea shell",0],["Fossil fern leaf",1],["Dinosaur footprint",1],["Mammoth tooth",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfossev","caption":"Flagship lab: order rock layers by age, match fossils to where they lived, push a seabed up into a mountain, dig up horse feet, then read a cliff.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_M8UQTURODF":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"An artist is starting a new dinosaur picture. What should they draw first?","choices":["The skeleton","The colour pattern","The feathers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with the strongest evidence.","explain":"The skeleton comes from real fossils. Muscles, skin and colour are added on top, each a bit less certain."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which fossil could show the pattern of a dinosaur's scales?","choices":["A skin impression in rock","A tooth","A leg bone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which find is a print of the outside of the body?","explain":"Skin pressed into mud before it hardened can keep the shape of scales, even when the skin is gone."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What are melanosomes useful for?","choices":["Working out real colours","Measuring the skeleton","Finding how fast it ran"],"answer":0,"hint":"They are tiny sacs that hold colour in feathers and skin.","explain":"Their shapes match colours in living birds, so in rare fossils they reveal colour, like Sinosauropteryx's striped tail."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A book with a scaly Velociraptor was printed in {y}. Quill knobs were found in 2007. How many years before the find was the book printed?","answer":"2007 - y","hint":"Take the earlier year away from 2007.","explain":"2007 − {y} = {=2007-y} years. Before 2007 nobody had the evidence, so the scaly picture was a fair guess for its time.","params":{"y":[1960,2005]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A film shows Velociraptor taller than a grown-up. What do the fossils say?","choices":["It was about the size of a turkey","The film is right","Nobody knows its size"],"answer":0,"hint":"We have its bones, so we can measure it.","explain":"The skeleton shows a turkey-sized animal, up to about 2 m long with its tail. The film made it much bigger."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Is palaeoart just made up?","choices":["No: it starts from fossils and fills gaps with careful guesses","Yes, nobody saw a dinosaur","No, every part is proven"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about which layers come from fossils and which do not.","explain":"Bones and teeth are evidence. Colour and soft parts are informed guesses that must fit the evidence."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A model dinosaur has {e} parts built from fossil evidence and {g} parts that are guesses. How many parts are guesses?","answer":"g","hint":"Only count the parts that no fossil showed.","explain":"{g} parts are guesses. A good museum label says which ones, so visitors know.","params":{"e":[3,9],"g":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How do artists decide how big a dinosaur's muscles were?","choices":["From marks on the bones and from living animals","From its colour","They draw them any size"],"answer":0,"hint":"Muscles leave rough scars where they join bone.","explain":"Muscle scars on bones, plus birds and crocodiles alive today, guide the muscle layer."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which part of a dinosaur is most often an artist's guess?","choices":["Its skin colour","The shape of its teeth","The length of its leg bones"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which parts are hard enough to turn into stone?","explain":"Teeth and bones fossilise, so they can be measured. Colour almost never survives, so artists choose it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A dig finds {b} bones and {t} teeth from one dinosaur, but no skin. How many of these finds show its colour?","answer":"0","hint":"Do bones or teeth keep the colour of the skin?","explain":"0. None of the {b} bones or {t} teeth shows skin colour, so that part of the picture is still a guess.","params":{"b":[4,20],"t":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A fossil skeleton is {=2*h} metres long, nose to tail. Half of the length is tail. How many metres long is the tail?","answer":"h","hint":"Half of a length means divide it by 2.","explain":"{=2*h} ÷ 2 = {h} metres of tail. Measuring bones like this is real evidence, not a guess.","params":{"h":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"How do scientists know Velociraptor had feathers, when no feathers were found with it?","choices":["Quill knobs on its arm bone","A photo from long ago","Its teeth were sharp"],"answer":0,"hint":"Birds have small bumps on a bone where big feathers attach.","explain":"In 2007 scientists found quill knobs on a Velociraptor arm bone. They only form where large feathers are fixed."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why do pictures of the same dinosaur change over the years?","choices":["New fossils give new evidence","The dinosaur changed how it looked","Artists copy films"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did the dinosaur change, or what we know about it?","explain":"The dinosaur was the same all along. New finds, like quill knobs and feathered fossils, changed what we know."}],"explain":{"kid":["Nobody has ever seen a living dinosaur. So how do we have pictures of them? Scientists and artists work together. Their pictures are called palaeoart. Every good picture starts with fossils.","Hard parts last. Bones, teeth and claws can turn to stone, so we can measure them. Footprints can last too. Soft parts nearly always rot away first. That means skin, fat, muscle, eyes and colour are usually missing. Sounds leave nothing behind at all.","So artists build a dinosaur in layers. First the bones. Then muscles, using marks on the bones and animals alive today, like birds and crocodiles. Then skin or feathers. Colour comes last, and it is usually a guess. A careful guess is called speculation. It must fit the evidence, but it could be wrong.","Sometimes a rare fossil fills a gap. Skin pressed into mud can show scales. Bumps on an arm bone, called quill knobs, show where big feathers were fixed. In 2007 these were found on Velociraptor, so the old scaly pictures had to change. Tiny colour sacs in fossil feathers even showed that Sinosauropteryx had a striped ginger and white tail."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 11. In England this supports Year 6 science on evolution and inheritance. Pupils learn that fossils give us facts about living things from millions of years ago. It also fits working scientifically: finding evidence that supports or refutes an idea, and seeing how ideas change when new evidence turns up.","Useful terms. Palaeoart rebuilds extinct animals from fossils. Artists start with the skeleton. They add muscles using muscle scars and living relatives, birds and crocodiles. Then comes the body covering: scales, fuzz or feathers. Colour comes last. Rare fossils give direct evidence. Some Edmontosaurus fossils keep skin prints. A Velociraptor arm bone has quill knobs (Turner and others, 2007). Melanosomes showed the striped tail of Sinosauropteryx (Zhang and others, 2010).","Common mistakes to watch for. Many children think a museum model is a fact, like a photo. Others think it is all made up. Some think the dinosaurs changed when the pictures changed. A common slip is to trust films: film raptors are far bigger than the real, turkey-sized Velociraptor. Ask: which part could a fossil show, and which part did someone choose?"]},"familyTip":"Look at a dinosaur in a book, a toy or a film together. Point to each part and ask: would a fossil show this, or did someone choose it? Bones and teeth are evidence; colour and noises are nearly always guesses. Then look up a picture of Velociraptor from an old book and a new one, and spot what changed.","keyIdea":"Palaeoart is the science of drawing and modelling dinosaurs. It starts from fossils: bones, teeth, claws and footprints are real evidence. Skin colour, fat, muscles and sounds usually do not fossilise, so artists make informed guesses using living relatives such as birds and crocodiles. Rare finds fill some gaps: skin impressions show scales, quill knobs show feathers, and melanosomes can show real colour. When new fossils turn up, the pictures change, which is why Velociraptor went from scaly to feathered.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Evidence or guess? Drag each part of a dinosaur picture, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fossils show it","Artists must guess"],"items":[["Leg bones",0],["Skin colour",1],["Teeth",0],["Its roar",1],["Footprints",0],["Eye colour",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpalart","caption":"Flagship lab: sort fossil evidence from guesses, build a dinosaur in layers, drag through four discoveries, match rare clues and check a museum label.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_uQljqc0J5j":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which period came first?","choices":["Triassic","Jurassic","Cretaceous"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remember T, J, C. Which period held the very first, small dinosaurs?","explain":"The Triassic was first, from about 252 to 201 million years ago. The first dinosaurs lived in it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which period lasted the longest?","choices":["Cretaceous","Jurassic","Triassic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which coloured block on the time line was the widest?","explain":"The Cretaceous lasted about 79 million years, longer than the Jurassic (about 56) or the Triassic (about 51)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In which period did Stegosaurus live?","choices":["Jurassic","Triassic","Cretaceous"],"answer":0,"hint":"Stegosaurus lived about 150 million years ago. Which period covers that?","explain":"The Jurassic ran from about 201 to 145 million years ago, so 150 million years ago is late in the Jurassic."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Did people live at the same time as dinosaurs like T. rex?","choices":["No, people came tens of millions of years later","Yes, people hunted them","Yes, but only at the end"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where was the 'today' mark on the time line?","explain":"The first people lived about 300,000 years ago. T. rex died out 66 million years ago, long before."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How did the Mesozoic Era end?","choices":["An asteroid hit Earth and most dinosaurs died out","The dinosaurs got too big","It has not ended"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happened 66 million years ago?","explain":"About 66 million years ago a huge asteroid hit Earth. Most dinosaurs died out, and only the birds lived on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Triceratops and T. rex both lived about 68 to 66 million years ago. Could they have met?","choices":["Yes, their times overlap","No, they lived in different periods","No, Triceratops was Triassic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the two time bars overlap?","explain":"Both lived at the end of the Cretaceous, so their bars overlap. Fossils of both are found in the same rocks."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is the Mesozoic?","choices":["An era made of three periods","One kind of dinosaur","A place where fossils are found"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the three coloured blocks together.","explain":"The Mesozoic is an era, a long stretch of time, made of the Triassic, the Jurassic and the Cretaceous."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"The Cretaceous began about 145 million years ago and ended 66 million years ago. A dinosaur lived {n} million years before the end. How many million years ago was that?","answer":"66+n","params":{"n":[2,70]},"hint":"Before the end means longer ago. Add {n} to 66.","explain":"66 + {n} = {=66+n}, so it lived about {=66+n} million years ago, inside the Cretaceous."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A period began {a} million years ago and ended {b} million years ago. How many million years did it last?","answer":"a-b","params":{"a":[150,252],"b":[66,140]},"hint":"Take the end date away from the start date: {a} − {b}.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} million years. Bigger numbers mean longer ago."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which list is in order, from oldest to youngest?","choices":["Triassic, Jurassic, Cretaceous","Jurassic, Triassic, Cretaceous","Cretaceous, Jurassic, Triassic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think T, J, C. Which period had the very first dinosaurs?","explain":"The Triassic came first, then the Jurassic, then the Cretaceous, which ended 66 million years ago."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The Jurassic began about 201 million years ago and ended about 145 million years ago. A dinosaur lived {n} million years after the Jurassic began. How many million years ago was that?","answer":"201-n","params":{"n":[5,50]},"hint":"Count forward in time means fewer years ago. Take {n} away from 201.","explain":"201 − {n} = {=201-n}, so it lived about {=201-n} million years ago, still in the Jurassic."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"When did Tyrannosaurus rex live?","choices":["At the end of the Cretaceous","In the Jurassic","All through the Mesozoic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where did you drop T. rex on the time line in the lab?","explain":"T. rex lived about 68 to 66 million years ago, right at the end of the Cretaceous, just before the asteroid hit."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Stegosaurus lived about 150 million years ago. Another animal lived {n} million years ago. How many million years apart are they?","answer":"150-n","params":{"n":[67,140]},"hint":"Find the gap between the two dates: take {n} away from 150.","explain":"150 − {n} = {=150-n} million years apart. A bar that ends before the other starts means they never met."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Could Stegosaurus and T. rex have met?","choices":["No, they lived about 83 million years apart","Yes, they lived at the same time","Yes, but only in the Triassic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the two gaps you measured in the lab.","explain":"Stegosaurus lived about 150 million years ago and T. rex about 67. The gap is about 83 million years, more than from T. rex to us."}],"explain":{"kid":["The time of the dinosaurs is called the Mesozoic Era. It began about 252 million years ago and ended 66 million years ago. That is a huge stretch of time, so it is split into three parts, called periods.","The periods go in this order: Triassic, Jurassic, Cretaceous. Say T, J, C to remember it. The first dinosaurs lived in the Triassic. They were small and fast. The Cretaceous was the last period and the longest one.","Dinosaurs did not all live at the same time. Stegosaurus lived in the Jurassic. T. rex lived at the very end of the Cretaceous. The time between them is longer than the time between T. rex and you! So they never met.","No people lived in the Mesozoic. The era ended when a huge rock from space hit Earth. Most dinosaurs died out. Birds are the dinosaurs that lived on."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. This supports the Key Stage 2 science topic on rocks and fossils and evolution in the National Curriculum in England (Years 3 and 6) and NGSS 3-LS4-1 (fossils as evidence of organisms and environments of long ago). Dates are rounded from the International Chronostratigraphic Chart (2023); dinosaur dates are from the Natural History Museum Dino Directory. Key words: era, period, Mesozoic, Triassic, Jurassic, Cretaceous, million years ago, time line, extinction, fossil.","The lab has five tasks on one time line drawn to scale, from 252 million years ago to today. Children put the periods in order, then measure them: about 51, 56 and 79 million years. They drag five dinosaurs into their periods. They tap marks to measure two gaps: about 83 million years from Stegosaurus to T. rex, and 67 from T. rex to today. Last, they judge five pairs, using whether the two time bars overlap.","Common mistakes. Children often think all dinosaurs lived together, so pictures that show Stegosaurus fighting T. rex feel right. Other slips: the Jurassic was the longest or the only dinosaur period, the order is Jurassic, Triassic, Cretaceous, people and dinosaurs lived together, and a dinosaur kind lasted the whole era. Be careful with big numbers: 66 million years is far longer than all of human history, so a scaled time line helps more than words."]},"familyTip":"Make a time line along a hallway or a long strip of paper: one big step or 10 cm for every 10 million years. Mark today at one end, then 66, 145, 201 and 252 million years ago. Put a toy or a drawing of each dinosaur at its time and see how far apart Stegosaurus and T. rex really are.","keyIdea":"The Mesozoic Era, from about 252 to 66 million years ago, is the age of dinosaurs. It has three periods in order: the Triassic (252 to 201 million years ago), the Jurassic (201 to 145) and the Cretaceous (145 to 66). Different dinosaurs lived in different periods, not all at the same time: Coelophysis lived in the Triassic, Stegosaurus and Brachiosaurus in the Jurassic, and Tyrannosaurus rex and Triceratops at the end of the Cretaceous. Stegosaurus lived about 83 million years before T. rex, a bigger gap than the 66 million years between T. rex and us. No people lived in the Mesozoic.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which period did each one live in? Drag each card, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Triassic","Jurassic","Cretaceous"],"items":[["Coelophysis, one of the first dinosaurs",0],["Plateosaurus, an early long-necked plant-eater",0],["Stegosaurus, with plates on its back",1],["Brachiosaurus, a giant long neck",1],["Tyrannosaurus rex",2],["Triceratops, with three horns",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmeso","caption":"Flagship lab: order the three periods, measure them on a scaled time line, place five dinosaurs, measure the T. rex gap, and judge who could meet.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_QHKqckBdAk":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"About how long ago did the K-Pg extinction happen?","choices":["66 million years ago","66 thousand years ago","6,600 years ago"],"answer":0,"hint":"The clay layer and the Chicxulub crater are the same age. What was it?","explain":"It happened about 66 million years ago, at the end of the Cretaceous period, long before any humans lived."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which metal in the thin clay layer was the first big clue to an asteroid?","choices":["Iridium","Iron","Gold"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which metal is rare in Earth's crust but common in asteroids?","explain":"Iridium. The Alvarez team found about 30 times more of it in the clay than in the rock around it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Where is the Chicxulub crater?","choices":["Under the Yucatán Peninsula in Mexico","In Arizona, USA","In the middle of Africa"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, which crater was 66 million years old and 180 km wide?","explain":"Chicxulub is buried under the Yucatán Peninsula and the sea floor next to it, in Mexico."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which of these groups is still alive today?","choices":["Birds","Pterosaurs","Ammonites"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which group of dinosaurs did not die out?","explain":"Birds are dinosaurs that survived. Pterosaurs and ammonites died out completely."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Put these in order: A plants die back, B the Sun is blocked, C plant-eaters starve. Which order is right?","choices":["B, A, C","A, B, C","C, B, A"],"answer":0,"hint":"Plants need light. Plant-eaters need plants.","explain":"The Sun is blocked, then plants die back, then the animals that eat plants starve."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Sam says the asteroid killed every living thing. What is wrong?","choices":["About a quarter of kinds of living things survived, such as birds and small mammals","Nothing, every living thing died","Only plants survived"],"answer":0,"hint":"If everything died, where would today's animals come from?","explain":"About three quarters of species died out. The rest, including birds, frogs, turtles and small mammals, lived on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which animal had the best chance of surviving the dark, cold years after the impact?","choices":["A small burrowing mammal that ate insects and seeds","A huge plant-eater that ate fresh leaves","A big meat-eater that hunted plant-eaters"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which food lasted when plants stopped growing? Where could an animal shelter?","explain":"Small animals need little food, seeds and insects lasted in the dark, and burrows gave shelter. Big animals ran out of food."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In a cliff, which rock layer formed first?","choices":["The bottom layer","The top layer","The middle layer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each new layer settles on top of the ones already there.","explain":"Layers build up from the bottom, so the bottom layer formed first and is the oldest."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"The asteroid was about 10 km wide. A school bus is about 10 metres long, and 1 km is 1,000 metres. How many buses end to end would match {k} km?","answer":"k*100","params":{"k":[1,10]},"hint":"1 km = 1,000 m, so 100 buses of 10 m fit in 1 km. Multiply {k} by 100.","explain":"{k} × 100 = {=k*100} buses. Ten km, the width of the asteroid, is 1,000 buses."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"For every 1 kind of living thing that survived, about 3 kinds died out. In a group where {n} kinds survived, about how many kinds died out?","answer":"3*n","params":{"n":[2,12]},"hint":"Three died out for every one that lived. Work out 3 × {n}.","explain":"3 × {n} = {=3*n} kinds died out. That is the three quarters that went extinct."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In a cliff, dinosaur bones are found in the layers below a thin clay layer but never above it. What does that tell us?","choices":["Dinosaurs died out around the time the clay layer formed","Dinosaurs lived after the clay layer formed","The clay layer is the oldest rock in the cliff"],"answer":0,"hint":"Lower layers are older. Where do the dinosaur bones stop?","explain":"Layers below the clay are older and have dinosaurs; layers above are younger and have none. So the dinosaurs died out when the clay formed."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A rock has {r} units of iridium. The boundary clay has 30 times as much. How many units does the clay have?","answer":"30*r","params":{"r":[1,9]},"hint":"Times as much means multiply. Work out {r} × 30.","explain":"{r} × 30 = {=30*r} units. A jump that big in one thin layer is what pointed scientists to an asteroid."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A dinosaur bone comes from a layer {a} million years old. The K-Pg clay layer is 66 million years old. How many million years before the extinction did this dinosaur live?","answer":"a-66","params":{"a":[67,99]},"hint":"Older layers have bigger ages. Take 66 away from {a}.","explain":"{a} − 66 = {=a-66}, so it lived {=a-66} million years before the extinction. Its layer sits lower in the cliff than the clay."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why did Triceratops die out, even though the asteroid hit far away in Mexico?","choices":["Dust and soot blocked the Sun, so the plants it ate died back","The asteroid landed right on every Triceratops","It was too slow to run from the crater"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the food chain step in the lab. What does a plant-eater need?","explain":"Without sunlight, plants could not make food. Big plant-eaters like Triceratops starved, and the meat-eaters that hunted them starved next."}],"explain":{"kid":["Rock forms in layers, one on top of another. So the deepest layers are the oldest. In cliffs from the end of the age of dinosaurs, there are dinosaur bones in the low layers. Then there is a thin layer of clay. Above it, there are no dinosaur bones at all. Something big happened at that line, 66 million years ago.","In 1980, Luis Alvarez and his son Walter tested that clay in Italy. It held about 30 times more iridium than the rock around it. Iridium is a metal that is rare in Earth's rocks but common in asteroids. They said a huge asteroid must have hit Earth. Later, a crater about 180 km wide was found under the Yucatán Peninsula in Mexico. It is called Chicxulub, and it is 66 million years old.","The asteroid was about 10 km wide. It threw hot rock into the sky and started fires. Dust, soot and sulfur blocked the Sun for months or years. It got dark and cold. Plants could not make food, so plant-eaters starved, and then the meat-eaters did too.","About three out of every four kinds of living things died out. All the big dinosaurs went, and so did pterosaurs, mosasaurs and ammonites. Small animals that ate seeds, insects or dead things did best. Birds lived on, and birds are dinosaurs. So are the pigeons and robins you see today."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 11. This fits Key Stage 2 science in the National Curriculum in England (Year 3 rocks and fossils; Year 6 evolution and inheritance: fossils give information about living things from millions of years ago) and NGSS 4-ESS1-1 (patterns in rock layers and fossils show changes over time) and 3-LS4-1 (fossils show organisms and environments of long ago). Key words: extinction, mass extinction, fossil, rock layer (stratum), sedimentary rock, Cretaceous, Palaeogene, asteroid, impact crater, iridium, evidence, food chain, producer, consumer.","The lab has six tasks. Children dig down a cliff like those at Hell Creek, Montana, and find the thin clay where dinosaur fossils stop. They move a probe through the layers and find the iridium spike in the clay (about 30 times background, as at Gubbio). They drop the asteroid on the crater that fits by age and size: Chicxulub, not Meteor Crater (1.2 km, 50,000 years) or Manicouagan (214 million years). They put the impact-winter chain in order, sort eight groups into died out and survived, and build an animal with survivor traits.","Common mistakes. Many children think the asteroid hit every dinosaur directly; most died later, when food chains collapsed. Others think no dinosaurs survived, but birds are living dinosaurs, while pterosaurs (which died out) were flying reptiles, not dinosaurs. Children also mix up the order of layers (the top is youngest) and think dinosaurs and early humans lived together. Be careful with 'all life died': about three quarters of species died out, so a quarter survived. Huge volcanic eruptions in India (the Deccan Traps) happened around the same time, and scientists still discuss how much they added."]},"familyTip":"Stack a few slices of different bread or coloured play dough to make rock layers, and push a toy dinosaur into a low layer. Ask which layer went down first and which is oldest. Then look at a world map together and find the Yucatán Peninsula in Mexico, where the crater is buried. If you have a garden bird feeder, talk about how the birds visiting it are living dinosaurs.","keyIdea":"About 66 million years ago, at the end of the Cretaceous period, an asteroid about 10 km wide hit Earth. It made the Chicxulub crater, about 180 km wide, now buried under the Yucatán Peninsula in Mexico. Dust, soot and sulfur blocked sunlight, so plants died back and food chains broke. About three quarters of all species died out, including every dinosaur except the birds. The evidence includes a thin clay layer found round the world, rich in iridium (a metal common in asteroids but rare in Earth's crust), with shocked quartz and glass beads, and dinosaur fossils only below it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Did each group die out or live on after the K-Pg extinction? Drag each card, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Died out","Lived on"],"items":[["Triceratops",0],["Ammonites",0],["Mosasaurs",0],["Birds",1],["Frogs",1],["Small mammals",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagkpg","caption":"Flagship lab: dig to where dinosaur fossils stop, probe for iridium, match the crater, order the food-chain collapse, sort survivors and build one.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
